terResource1_8.save` (`omod/.../openmrs1_8/EncounterResource1_8.java:190`) returns `Context.getEncounterService().saveEncounter(enc)`. This is the base encounter resource's create/update path, and `EncounterResource1_9` and `EncounterResource2_2` inherit it.\n2. `EncounterResource1_9.save` (`omod/.../openmrs1_9/EncounterResource1_9.java:84`) sets the back-reference on each `EncounterProvider`, then calls `saveEncounter(delegate)` and returns the delegate. Its comment says the hack exists so encounter providers get an `encounter_id`, so a behaviour change in `saveEncounter` (cascade, flush, or return value) would hit this directly.\n3. `EncounterProviderResource1_9.save` (`omod/.../openmrs1_9/EncounterProviderResource1_9.java:186`) adds the provider to the encounter, calls `saveEncounter(delegate.getEncounter())`, then re-fetches the persisted provider by role and provider. It depends on `saveEncounter` persisting the new `EncounterProvider` and on the encounter's provider set being updated.\n4. `EncounterProviderResource1_9.delete` (`omod/.../openmrs1_9/EncounterProviderResource1_9.java:174`) voids the provider row and then calls `saveEncounter` on the parent encounter to persist the void.\n\n**Test code (confirmed)**\n\n- `EncounterController1_9Test` calls `saveEncounter` after `voidEncounter` at lines 532 and 553, and the assertions depend on the voided-encounter counts.\n- `OrderController1_10Test` calls `es.saveEncounter(encounter)` at line 355 to create an encounter before revising an order.\n\n**Likely but not verified**\n\n- The generic `DelegatingCrudResource` create/update path should call `save()` on these resources. I did not read that base class, so I did not confirm the dispatch chain.\n- `ObsResource1_8` (line 572) and `MedicationDispenseResource2_6` (line 404) look up encounters through `EncounterResource1_8.getByUniqueId`, not `saveEncounter`. They would only be affected if the lookup behaviour changed, so I list them as weak.\n\n**Not checked**\n\n-